Actually, the real underlying issue is the "do"-ification of verbs in
question sentences (and negations too for that matter).
So when you make a question, normally the rule is to "do"-ify the
verb:

"You eat floating-eye corpses." =3D> "Do you eat floating-eye corpses?"
"You killed the kobold." =3D> "Did you kill the kobold?"

However, the rule doesn't apply to "to be", auxiliary verbs (like
"have" when making the past perfect tense), and some others ("can",
"may", etc):

"You are poison-resistant." =3D> "Are you poison-resistant?" not "Do you
be poison-resistant?"
"You have inspected." =3D> "Have you inspected?" not "Do you have
inspected?"
I believe that this was source of Janis's confusion.

"Have" can be tricky since it can be do-ified when it is a regular
verb (at least in American dialects), but not when it is an auxiliary
verb.
"Do you have any zorkmids?" is correct, but "Do you have seen any
zorkmids?" isn't.

English: we might not have many verbal inflections, but we can still
make it hard to learn.



